  "howToStep1Title": "Create a project",
  "howToStep1Body": "Tap New app and choose HTML files, a website ZIP, or a live https website URL. Files and ZIPs work offline; a URL needs an internet connection.",
  "howToStep2Title": "Name your app",
  "howToStep2Body": "Enter the app name and a unique package ID such as com.yourname.app. Keep the package ID the same for every update.",
  "howToStep3Title": "Customize it",
  "howToStep3Body": "In the Settings tab choose the start page, icon, splash screen, colours, orientation, shortcuts and WebView features. Tap Save when you are done. Use Preview to try the app first.",
  "howToStep4Title": "Build",
  "howToStep4Body": "In the Build tab choose Debug to test, or Release to publish. Release builds need a release key: create one and back it up. On Windows you can also build a .exe or an .msix installer.",
  "howToStep5Title": "Install or share",
  "howToStep5Body": "Install the APK on this phone, share it, or save it. On a PC use Install on phone to send it over Wi-Fi. Upload App Bundles (.aab) to Google Play Console.",
  "howToTipsTitle": "Tips",
  "howToTips": "• Every update must use the same package ID and release key, and a higher version code.\n• Back up your release keys and projects; nobody can recover a lost key.\n• Use the Size report to make the app smaller.\n• Use Back up project (top of a project) to keep a copy you can restore later.",
  "termsBody": "These Terms of Use apply to the iLoveHTML app (\"the app\"), made by 1elevenop. By using the app you agree to them.\n\n## What the app does\n\niLoveHTML turns HTML files, website ZIPs and website addresses into Android and Windows apps. Builds run on your own device.\n\n## Your content\n\nYou keep all rights to the websites, files, icons and apps you create. You are responsible for having the right to use that content, and for the apps you build and publish, including following the law and the rules of app stores such as Google Play.\n\n## Acceptable use\n\nDo not use the app to build software that is illegal, harmful, deceptive, or that infringes the rights of others, such as malware or apps that impersonate other brands.\n\n## Release keys\n\nRelease keys are stored only on your device. You are responsible for backing them up and keeping their passwords safe. A lost key cannot be recovered, and apps signed with it can then no longer be updated.\n\n## No warranty\n\nThe app is provided free of charge and \"as is\", without warranties of any kind. To the extent permitted by law, the author is not liable for any loss of data, keys, income or other damages arising from its use.\n\n## Changes\n\nThese terms may be updated with new versions of the app. The date above shows the latest change. Continuing to use the app means you accept the updated terms.\n\n## Contact\n\nQuestions: open an issue on the iLoveHTML GitHub project by 1elevenop.",
  "privacyBody": "iLoveHTML is built to work on your device. This policy explains what that means for your data.\n\n## Data we collect\n\nNone. The app has no accounts, no analytics, no advertising and no tracking. The author receives no data about you or your projects.\n\n## Data stored on your device\n\nYour projects, website files, icons, build outputs and settings are stored in the app's private storage on your device. Release keys stay on your device too: on Android they are encrypted with the Android Keystore, and on Windows their passwords are encrypted for your Windows account. Uninstalling the app deletes this data, so keep backups.\n\n## Network use\n\nThe app connects to the internet only when you ask it to: to check or preview a website URL you entered, to open links such as Buy Me a Coffee in your browser, and, on Windows, when Gradle downloads build tools. The Install on phone feature serves the file on your local network for 10 minutes, only while its window is open.\n\n## Update check\n\nIf \"Check for updates\" is on (it is by default), about twice a day iLoveHTML asks GitHub (api.github.com) for the latest iLoveHTML release, to tell you when a new version is out. The request contains no account, no identifiers and nothing about your projects; like any website, GitHub sees your IP address. Turn it off in Settings → About.\n\n## Apps you build\n\nApps made with iLoveHTML contain no tracking from iLoveHTML. What they collect depends on the website you put in them, and you are responsible for their privacy policy.\n\n## Children\n\nThe app does not knowingly collect any data, from children or anyone else.\n\n## Changes and contact\n\nThis policy may be updated with new versions of the app; the date above shows the latest change. Questions: open an issue on the iLoveHTML GitHub project by 1elevenop.",
